PROJECTS

We have four main strategies

    POLICY & REGULATORY ADVOCACY: We create spaces within national and international telecommunications regulatory frameworks to allow for community-based communications initiatives to thrive and grow.

    TECHNOLOGY DEVELOPMENT: We integrate and develop free and open-source software to facilitate ease-of use for community or small-scale deployments of autonomous GSM networks and digital HF networks. We also develop and innovate appropriate energy technologies that support circular bioeconomies in rural and indigenous communities. 

 
    CAPACITY BUILDING AND IMPLEMENTATION: We have created a proven model of how to make community-owned communication networks a sustainable endeavor for the community itself. We work directly with community members and social organizations around the world to ensure they know how to operate, maintain and troubleshoot equipment, as well as how to use the technology for community benefit.


    RESEARCH AND KNOWLEDGE PRODUCTION: We gather and produce knowledge that informs the other areas of our work in order to promote and strengthen community and indigenous communication and telecommunications, and appropriate technologies for circular bioeconomies.
    

Our Projects

HERMES - The High-frequency Emergency and Rural Multimedia Exchange System, better known by its acronym, HERMES, provides affordable and easy-to-use digital telecommunications over shortwave/HF radio, allowing for the transmission and reception of data (chat, audio, documents, photos, GPS coordinates, etc). For security, this information can be easily encrypted and password-protected r. HERMES, both architecture designs and software, is free and open-source. Learn more about HERMES [add links to hermes.radio, mailing list, github, mercury]

LocNet - Local Networks community-centered connectivity Initiative. The Local Networks (LocNet) initative started in 2017 as a collaboration between APC and Rhizomatica. LocNet’s foundational belief is in the power of community-centred connectivity to catalyse positive change and bridge the digital divide by supporting thriving, inclusive and meaningful community-centred connectivity in rural, remote and marginalised communities. Through LocNet, we support communities with technology and capacity building and advocate for more inclusive policies and gender inclusion in approximately 20 Global Majority countries. More information can be found at [https://www.apc.org/en/our-work/themes/digital-inclusion]

Capacity-building - In 2016 we co-facilitated the creation of the Techio Comunitario training program for community-based ICT network operators, aimed at people belonging to indigenous and rural communities in Latin America to provide technical knowledge for the installation, operation and maintenance of communication projects and create a community of practice of people who accompany each other to strengthen the path towards technological autonomy in their territories. Over the years, we have built upon this process, later creating the Training Programme for Technical Promoters and Promoters in Telecommunications and Broadcasting in Indigenous Communities, developed in collaboration with the International Telecommunications Union (ITU), with editions in Latin America and Africa. [https://cnlearning.apc.org/] [https://techiocomunitario.org/en/]

CITSAC - Center for Research in Technology and Community Knowledge
CITSAC is a space where the production and exchange of knowledge is promoted, and community capacities and processes aimed at engendering buen vivir (living well) are enhanced. CITSAC promotes processes to strengthen community-based knowledge recovery and generation, grounded in the reality that local communities face constant challenges in preserving and revitalizing their ancestral knowledge; this approach seeks not only to rescue and keep traditional practices and knowledge alive, but also to foster the creation of new knowledge that addresses the communities’ current needs. [citsac.org]


